    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe composition itself is placed and structured well: `riskFlag` sits with its sibling derived `default` methods in `OwnerMapper` in the same order as the `@Mapping` declarations, reuses the existing `possibleDuplicate`/`capacityWarning` methods rather than re-reading the raw fields, and delegates the email logic to `EmailNormalizer` \u2014 the class that already owns `DISPOSABLE_DOMAINS` and email parsing. That's the right unit for each piece and matches the established normalizer-delegation idiom in this file. One coherence issue:\n\n- **`riskFlag` javadoc has a copy-pasted, inaccurate sentence** (`OwnerMapper.java:297-299`). It ends with \"A `null` stored value on either flag is treated as `false`, so the field is always present in the response.\" That boilerplate is lifted verbatim from `bulkSignupWarning`/`capacityWarning`/`possibleDuplicate`, which each guard a single *stored* `owner.getX()` value. `riskFlag` has no stored value \u2014 it's a pure disjunction over three derived signals \u2014 so \"a null stored value on either flag\" (which flag? there are three inputs, and one isn't even a boolean field) doesn't describe this method and will mislead a maintainer. Drop or rewrite that trailing sentence to reflect that the result is always non-null (except for the `null`-owner case) because it composes the three sub-flags.\n\nEverything else reads as natural for this codebase; no duplication or catch-all concerns beyond the doc.",
